<mujoco model="g1_29dof_locked_wrists">
  <!-- g1.xml with the six wrist joints held at zero by equality constraints, matching
       mj-nlp's models/unitree_g1/g1_29dof_locked_wrists.xml.

       nq/nv/nu, the joint order and the actuator order are unchanged, so a tape, a
       reference clip and an LQR gain schedule built against g1.xml all still index
       correctly. Replay a tape solved under this lock on the unlocked model and the six
       wrist commands ask for motion the constraint was absorbing: on the sideroll clip
       that costs 844 frames of 844 down to 171.

       The constraint is COMPLIANT, not a weld. At the default solref 0.02 1 the wrists
       still travel ~0.117 rad under it, which is what the solve's own reference for those
       joints is, so the wrist actuators keep real authority through it. Do not read this
       as pinning them rigidly, and do not zero the tape's wrist commands to compensate.

       polycoef="0 0 0 0 0" with joint2 omitted means "hold joint1 at its own qpos0", and
       every wrist joint here has no ref attribute, so qpos0 = 0.

       solref is left at the default 0.02 1. The time constant must be >= 2*timestep for a
       stable constraint; mjlab's tracking tasks run at 0.005, so 0.02 has margin. Tighten
       solimp, not solref, if the residual motion is too large. -->
  <include file="g1.xml"/>
  <equality>
    <joint name="lock/left_wrist_roll" joint1="left_wrist_roll_joint" polycoef="0 0 0 0 0"/>
    <joint name="lock/left_wrist_pitch" joint1="left_wrist_pitch_joint" polycoef="0 0 0 0 0"/>
    <joint name="lock/left_wrist_yaw" joint1="left_wrist_yaw_joint" polycoef="0 0 0 0 0"/>
    <joint name="lock/right_wrist_roll" joint1="right_wrist_roll_joint" polycoef="0 0 0 0 0"/>
    <joint name="lock/right_wrist_pitch" joint1="right_wrist_pitch_joint" polycoef="0 0 0 0 0"/>
    <joint name="lock/right_wrist_yaw" joint1="right_wrist_yaw_joint" polycoef="0 0 0 0 0"/>
  </equality>
</mujoco>
